1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,545
May twenty seventh, nineteen ninety one. Milwaukee, Wisconsin.

2
00:00:03,545 --> 00:00:11,174
Three women find a fourteen year old boy in the street, undressed and bleeding, and they call the police.

3
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:13,277
Two officers arrive.

4
00:00:13,277 --> 00:00:19,143
A man tells them the boy is nineteen, and his boyfriend, and that this is a domestic argument.

5
00:00:20,266 --> 00:00:23,042
The boy is not nineteen. He is fourteen.

6
00:00:23,042 --> 00:00:29,983
And the man is on probation, at that moment, for sexually assaulting a thirteen year old boy three years earlier.

7
00:00:31,133 --> 00:00:33,850
The officers hand the boy back to him.

8
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:40,302
In the words of a published appeals court opinion, they released Sinthasomphone, who was a minor, into Dahmer's care.

9
00:00:41,433 --> 00:00:45,456
The next sentence of that opinion is seven words long.

10
00:00:45,456 --> 00:00:47,467
Dahmer murdered Sinthasomphone shortly thereafter.

11
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:51,217
This film is not about what Jeffrey Dahmer did.

12
00:00:51,217 --> 00:00:54,708
That has been told a thousand times, and it needs no retelling.

13
00:00:54,708 --> 00:00:59,362
This is about the documents that came afterward, and what they show a system will forgive.

14
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:08,049
To understand the night of May twenty seventh, you have to start in nineteen eighty eight, with a different boy and the same surname.

15
00:01:09,166 --> 00:01:16,231
In nineteen eighty eight, Dahmer was arrested for drugging and sexually assaulting a thirteen year old boy named Somsack Sinthasomphone.

16
00:01:16,231 --> 00:01:17,290
He was convicted.

17
00:01:18,433 --> 00:01:21,986
He was not sent to prison for it. He was placed on probation.

18
00:01:23,133 --> 00:01:32,720
Three years later, still on that probation, he was standing in a Milwaukee street explaining to two officers that the fourteen year old beside him was an adult.

19
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:35,802
That fourteen year old was Somsack's younger brother, Konerak.

20
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:41,609
What happened in the next twenty minutes is described, dryly, in a court record.

21
00:01:42,733 --> 00:01:45,319
The opinion says the officers conducted a brief investigation.

22
00:01:45,319 --> 00:01:51,641
It says they released a minor into the care of the man who had just been found with him in the street.

23
00:01:52,766 --> 00:01:55,162
It does not say they knew what he was.

24
00:01:55,162 --> 00:01:59,688
There is nothing in this record that says they knew, and this film will not pretend otherwise.

25
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:09,035
What the record does establish is that a fourteen year old was handed back, and that a police department later concluded its own rules had been broken in the process.

26
00:02:10,166 --> 00:02:16,122
In July of nineteen ninety one, Dahmer was arrested, and what had happened that night in May became public.

27
00:02:17,266 --> 00:02:23,457
The then chief of police discharged the officers involved, for violating department rules during that investigation.

28
00:02:24,599 --> 00:02:28,100
The officers appealed to the city's Fire and Police Commission.

29
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:29,850
The commission upheld the discharges.

30
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,847
At that point, in the eyes of both the department and its civilian oversight body, the matter was settled.

31
00:02:36,847 --> 00:02:38,079
It was not settled.

32
00:02:39,199 --> 00:02:43,704
What happened next was not a jury, and not a public argument about policing.

33
00:02:43,704 --> 00:02:45,312
It was a section number.

34
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:57,431
Wisconsin statute sixty two point fifty governs how a police officer may be discharged, suspended, or reduced in rank, and what a court must do when it reviews that decision.

35
00:02:58,566 --> 00:03:01,262
The statute is narrow, and it is procedural.

36
00:03:01,262 --> 00:03:08,675
It says that where the accused is not permanently discharged, the officer shall be immediately reinstated in his or her former position.

37
00:03:09,799 --> 00:03:13,780
On review, the discharges were vacated and converted into suspensions.

38
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:18,158
Under the statute, that meant reinstatement, and it meant back pay.

39
00:03:19,299 --> 00:03:22,225
Reported at roughly fifty five thousand dollars each.

40
00:03:23,366 --> 00:03:27,916
That is the whole mechanism. Not a finding that the officers had acted properly.

41
00:03:27,916 --> 00:03:31,490
A statute about the difference between a discharge and a suspension.

42
00:03:32,633 --> 00:03:40,182
What happened after the reinstatement is the part of this record that almost nobody has read, and it is the part that says the most.

43
00:03:41,299 --> 00:03:44,996
The circuit court that reviewed the discharges called them unreasonable discipline.

44
00:03:44,996 --> 00:03:53,733
It sent the matter back, suggesting that a suspension of not more than sixty days would, in its words, more appropriately serve the ends of justice.

45
00:03:54,866 --> 00:04:00,069
Sixty days is not an arbitrary number. It is the ceiling written into the statute.

46
00:04:00,069 --> 00:04:09,782
Under section sixty two point fifty, a board that sustains the charges may discharge an officer permanently, or suspend without pay for a period not exceeding sixty days.

47
00:04:10,899 --> 00:04:15,394
So the commission vacated the discharges and imposed the maximum the law allowed.

48
00:04:15,394 --> 00:04:16,777
Sixty days without pay.

49
00:04:17,899 --> 00:04:21,452
And then the argument moved to what the word days means.

50
00:04:22,599 --> 00:04:26,747
The police department applied the suspension the way it always had.

51
00:04:26,747 --> 00:04:31,272
Sixty consecutive workdays, effective the twenty seventh of November, nineteen ninety two.

52
00:04:32,399 --> 00:04:34,109
The officer appealed that too.

53
00:04:34,109 --> 00:04:44,363
And the circuit court agreed with him, holding that days meant calendar days, and ordering the commission to redefine the suspension as sixty calendar days, with any lost rights restored.

54
00:04:45,499 --> 00:04:49,916
Working days, or calendar days. Roughly twelve weeks, or roughly eight.

55
00:04:49,916 --> 00:04:53,127
That difference is what the litigation was about.

56
00:04:54,266 --> 00:04:59,830
The Court of Appeals reversed, and held the department had it right. Sixty working days.

57
00:05:00,966 --> 00:05:05,551
Nineteen pages of statutory construction. A published opinion of a state appeals court.

58
00:05:05,551 --> 00:05:10,135
Filed in the year two thousand, about a night in nineteen ninety one.

59
00:05:11,266 --> 00:05:17,368
Nowhere in those nineteen pages does anyone argue about whether handing a fourteen year old boy back was wrong.

60
00:05:17,368 --> 00:05:22,185
That question was settled early, and quietly, and then everything after it was about arithmetic.

61
00:05:23,333 --> 00:05:27,931
The reinstatement was criticised immediately, and the criticism was specific.

62
00:05:29,066 --> 00:05:39,019
Many argued the investigation that night had been cursory because Konerak was not white, and because the officers accepted, without checking, that he was an adult in a relationship with the man beside him.

63
00:05:40,166 --> 00:05:45,554
That is a criticism, widely made, and it belongs in the record as exactly that.

64
00:05:45,554 --> 00:05:53,097
No court finding in these documents declares it as fact, and this film will not upgrade an accusation into a verdict.

65
00:05:54,233 --> 00:05:56,853
Konerak's family sued the city in federal court.

66
00:05:56,853 --> 00:06:02,749
The case is reported as Estate of Sinthasomphone against the City of Milwaukee, decided in nineteen ninety two.

67
00:06:03,866 --> 00:06:05,857
The civil side ran in parallel.

68
00:06:05,857 --> 00:06:13,819
Konerak's estate sued the city in federal court, and the reported decisions in that litigation span from nineteen ninety two into nineteen ninety five.

69
00:06:14,966 --> 00:06:20,564
We are not showing you those opinions, because we could not obtain clean copies of them for this episode.

70
00:06:20,564 --> 00:06:27,635
They exist, they are cited as Estate of Sinthasomphone against the City of Milwaukee, and when we have them, they will be on screen.

71
00:06:28,766 --> 00:06:30,600
That is the rule on this channel.

72
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:37,674
If we cannot put the document in front of you, we tell you it is missing rather than filling the gap with something that looks like it.

73
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:41,646
Dahmer's own case moved faster than any of it.

74
00:06:42,766 --> 00:06:45,310
He was charged with fifteen counts of murder.

75
00:06:45,310 --> 00:06:50,080
His defence did not dispute that he had killed. It argued that he was insane.

76
00:06:51,199 --> 00:07:00,447
On February fifteenth, nineteen ninety two, after roughly five hours, the jury rejected that argument and found him guilty on all fifteen counts.

77
00:07:01,566 --> 00:07:05,072
Two days later he was sentenced to fifteen consecutive life terms.

78
00:07:05,072 --> 00:07:09,533
In November of nineteen ninety four, he was killed by another inmate in prison.

79
00:07:10,666 --> 00:07:13,104
So here is the record, in one sentence.

80
00:07:13,104 --> 00:07:24,380
A man on probation for assaulting a thirteen year old was handed that boy's fourteen year old brother by two uniformed officers, and the paperwork that followed put those officers back on the payroll with back pay.

81
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:30,175
Every step of that sentence is in a filing you can read. None of it required a secret.

82
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:35,675
Three women called the police that night, and they were right about what they saw.

83
00:07:35,675 --> 00:07:38,301
The system's own paperwork is the part that failed.

84
00:07:39,433 --> 00:07:44,121
This is Criminal Record, where every case is built from the primary record and nothing else.

85
00:07:44,121 --> 00:07:51,447
If this one held you, the file we opened before it is a case where four students died and the record still contains no motive.

86
00:07:51,447 --> 00:07:52,912
Subscribe, and both are waiting.

